So the problem is, sensor networks pass data node to node, and some nodes are unreliable or even compromised — they drop or mess with packets  
Most routing just picks the shortest path and doesn't care if a node is trustworthy.  
What I built tracks each node's behavior over time, gives it a trust score, and locks that score on a lightweight blockchain so it can't be faked.  
Then routing picks paths through trusted nodes instead of just the shortest one — so you get fewer dropped packets and better security at the same time. 
So I have thought to solve the problem which is "Sensor networks lose data and get exploited because routing doesn't account for which nodes are actually trustworthy".
By the solution of building a trust-scoring system backed by a tamper-proof blockchain, so routing can dynamically avoid bad nodes.
Impact of this project: In simulation, this improved packet delivery and made the network resilient against nodes that start misbehaving mid-operation.
Algorithms used:
1	Erdős–Rényi random graph (gnp_random_graph)	network.py	Builds the random sensor network. Each pair of nodes is connected with probability 0.3.
2	Trust ratio Ti = Si / (Si + Fi)	trust.py	Turns each node’s success/fail counts into a trust score between 0 and 1.
3	BFS shortest path	routing.py	Baseline routing. Finds the path with the fewest hops and ignores trust.
4	Dijkstra’s algorithm with cost Ci = α·Di + β(1 − Ti)	routing.py	Trust-aware routing. Finds the path with the lowest combined distance-and-distrust cost.
5	SHA-256 hashing	blockchain.py	Hashes each block (node ID, trust value, timestamp, previous hash) to build the tamper-evident chain.
6	Hash-chain validation	blockchain.py	is_valid() walks the chain and checks that every block’s hash and previous-hash link are intact, which detects tampering.
7	Bernoulli (random) drop model	main.py	Simulates misbehaviour. Malicious nodes drop packets with 70% probability and normal nodes fail 5% of the time.
8	Random sampling	main.py	Picks which nodes are malicious and chooses random source/destination pairs for each packet.
